The closure uses `move ||` but doesn't declare `mut`. The closure type is inferred as Fn (not FnMut) because `move ||` defaults to Fn. With body that uses `&mut self` on captured `event_rx`, the compiler complains.

In Rust 2021, I can do:
```rust
move || { ... }
```
becomes Fn if doesn't mutate captured state. To make it FnMut:
```rust
move || { let _ = &mut event_rx; ... }
```
Or in newer syntax:
```rust
move || mut { ... }
```

Actually, looking at this more carefully: `glib::timeout_add_local` takes `impl FnMut`. The closure can be FnMut. We can force FnMut by binding a captured var to `mut`:
```rust
move || {
    let event_rx = &mut event_rx;
    while let Ok(event) = event_rx.try_recv() { ... }
}
```

Or simpler — use RefCell pattern:
```rust
let event_rx = Rc::new(RefCell::new(event_rx));
// closure:
move || {
    let mut rx = event_rx.borrow_mut();
    while let Ok(event) = rx.try_recv() { ... }
}
```

