parts.text:
OK no more gnome-ocr processes. Now let me think about why activate isn't firing.

Looking at my main function:
```rust
fn main() -> Result<()> {
    ...
    let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread().build()?;
    let _rt_guard = rt.enter();

    ...
    app.connect_activate(move |app| { ... });

    std::mem::forget(ipc_handle);
    std::mem::forget(daemon_handle);

    app.run();
    Ok(())
}
```

The `let _rt_guard = rt.enter();` returns a guard that exits the runtime when dropped. This guard is held until main returns. So during app.run(), we're "in" the tokio runtime.

But `app.run()` BLOCKS on the GTK main event loop. The tokio runtime's worker threads are running in the background. The IPC server's tokio::spawn is running on those workers.

The issue with activate not firing... let me look at this from a different angle. Maybe `Application::run()` doesn't fire activate if it thinks the app is not the primary instance.

Looking at gtk-rs docs: `app.run()` is equivalent to `g_application_run()`. Activation happens when the app is unique (D-Bus registration). If registration fails, it might exit early.

Let me check the actual behavior by removing the application_id:

