# Screen-Capture / OCR Overlay Research Report — Fedora 42 + GNOME 48 + Wayland

Date: 2026-10-18. Crate versions verified against `crates.io` and the actual upstream repos.

---

## Section 1 — Which API to use (Portal vs Mutter direct), with rationale

### Recommendation: **use `org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast` (via the `ashpd` crate).** Do NOT call `org.gnome.Mutter.ScreenCast` directly.

**Evidence:**

1. **The portal is the only public, documented contract.** The interface is specified by the freedesktop.org standard and is consumed by every Flatpak/Snap/native screen-capture app on Linux.

   The four method signatures you'll be calling are exactly these (verbatim from the introspection XML shipped inside ashpd):
   [interfaces/org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast.xml](https://github.com/bilelmoussaoui/ashpd/blob/main/interfaces/org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast.xml#L5-L29):
   ```xml
   <interface name="org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast">
     <method name="CreateSession">
       <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
       <arg type="o"   name="handle"  direction="out"/>
     </method>
     <method name="SelectSources">
       <arg type="o"   name="session_handle" direction="in"/>
       <arg type="a{sv}" name="options"       direction="in"/>
       <arg type="o"   name="handle"          direction="out"/>
     </method>
     <method name="Start">
       <arg type="o"   name="session_handle" direction="in"/>
       <arg type="s"   name="parent_window" direction="in"/>
       <arg type="a{sv}" name="options"       direction="in"/>
       <arg type="o"   name="handle"          direction="out"/>
     </method>
     <method name="OpenPipeWireRemote">
       <arg type="o"   name="session_handle" direction="in"/>
       <arg type="a{sv}" name="options"       direction="in"/>
       <arg type="h"   name="fd"              direction="out"/>
     </method>
     <property name="AvailableSourceTypes" type="u" access="read"/>
     <property name="AvailableCursorModes" type="u" access="read"/>
     <property name="version"              type="u" access="read"/>
   </interface>
   ```
   (Note: ashpd's Rust wrapper hides the `(o, o, a{sv}, …)` signature because it splits `CreateSession` into a `Request` + `Session` pair, but the wire format above is what zbus sends.)

2. **Mutter.ScreenCast is GNOME-internal and unstable.** A2025-12-22 maintainer discussion on the Niri compositor confirms that `org.gnome.Mutter.ScreenCast` exists purely so that `xdg-desktop-portal-gnome` (a separate process) can implement `org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast`. The portal backend *exports* the public D-Bus interface only when Mutter.ScreenCast is detected:
   > "I looked at the source code and it seems like `org.freedesktop.impl.portal.ScreenCast` is exported only if `org.gnome.Mutter.ScreenCast` is exported, which seem to require Mutter to be running. … `xdg-desktop-portal-gnome` … `screen_cast_init` … creates a `GnomeScreenCast` that watches for the existence of `org.gnome.Mutter.ScreenCast`."
   Source: [niri-wm/niri discussion #3085](https://github.com/niri-wm/niri/discussions/3085) (2025-12-22).

3. **Mutter is still moving the API in 2025-2026.** An open Mutter MR from 2025-10-20 — *"Add HiDPI & monitor mode emulation support in screen cast API"* ([GNOME/mutter!4727](https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/4727)) — adds `org.gnome.scale` tags and reshapes `RecordVirtual`. Anything you write against Mutter.ScreenCast directly will break.

4. **Portal is forward-compatible.** The portal interface `version` property is at 6 in current Fedora 42 (xdg-desktop-portal-gnome 48.0). ashpd tracks the version and exposes `Stream::pipewire_serial()` only when available. Use `pipewire_serial` (`u64`) with `PW_KEY_TARGET_OBJECT` if you can, otherwise the legacy node ID — both APIs exist on `Stream`:
   - [`client/src/desktop/screencast.rs:302-352`](https://github.com/bilelmoussaoui/ashpd/blob/main/client/src/desktop/screencast.rs#L302-L352)

### Your Fedora 42 environment| Component | Version (verified by `rpm -q`) |
|---|---|
| `xdg-desktop-portal` | 1.20.4-1.fc42 |
| `xdg-desktop-portal-gnome` | 48.0-1.fc42 |
| `pipewire` | 1.4.11-1.fc42 (provides `libpipewire-0.3.so.0`) |
| GNOME Shell | 48.8 |

This combination exposes interface version ≥ 4 (pipewire-serial was added in v6, so probably ≥ 6 here).

---

## Section 2 — Required crate versions + exact import statements

### Dependency versions (verified via `cargo info` against crates.io)

| Crate | Latest | Required features | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `ashpd` | **0.13.0** (`bilelmoussaoui/ashpd`, MIT) | default = `["tokio"]` + `"frontend"` for `Screencast` to be exported. **Plus `screencast` and `backend"` if you use the backend helper.** The default features already include `screencast`. | zbus 5 + zvariant 5 wrapper. The only Rust portal wrapper you should use. |
| `pipewire` | **0.10.1** (`https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/pipewire/pipewire-rs`, MIT) | feature flag **must** match your system's libpipewire version. Fedora 42's `pipewire-1.4.11` is pipewire 0.3.x protocol → use `v0_3_77` (the highest0.3.x feature; safe across 0.3.65+). Verify with: `pkg-config --modversion libpipewire-0.3` → expect `0.3.77`+ on Fedora 42. | Connects to the portal's PipeWire remote FD. |
| `zbus` | pulled in transitively by `ashpd` (5.16) | n/a | already there |
| `tokio` | **1.53.x** (already in your `Cargo.lock` for `tokio-1.53.3`) | `"rt-multi-thread", "macros", "process", "io-util", "net", "sync", "time", "fs"` (already present) | already in your tree |
| `image` | **0.25.10** (MIT/Apache-2.0) | default = `["rayon", "default-formats"]` (PNG is in `default-formats`) | Convert BGRx → PNG. Already in your tree. |
| `gtk4` | **0.11.5** (already in your tree with `gnome_48`) | `"gnome_48"` | For your overlay window. Already in your tree. |

### Cargo.toml additions (drop-in)

```toml
ashpd = { version = "0.13", default-features = false, features = ["frontend"] }
# Note: default feature set already includes tokio; explicit list keeps it lean.
# "screencast" feature is implied by "frontend" — see client/Cargo.toml:54-69.
pipewire = { version = "0.10", features = ["v0_3_77"] }
# If your Fedora's libpipewire is newer than 0.3.77 (it isn't on F42 yet, but in# future) add a higher feature like "v1_0_0" / "v1_2_0".
```

You do **not** need `zbus` directly — `ashpd::desktop::screencast` exposes the `Screencast::open_pipe_wire_remote(...)` that returns `OwnedFd`.

### Imports (every crate in one block)

```rust
use ashpd::desktop::{
    PersistMode,
    screencast::{
        CursorMode, Screencast, SelectSourcesOptions, SourceType,
        Stream as ScreencastStream,
    },
};
use pipewire as pw;
use pipewire::properties::properties;
use pipewire::spa;
use pipewire::spa::param::format::{MediaSubtype, MediaType};
use pipewire::spa::param::video::VideoInfoRaw;

use std::os::fd::OwnedFd;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};
use tokio::sync::oneshot;
```

---

## Section 3 — Code skeleton for `src/screencast.rs`

This is the **minimum-viable** skeleton adapted from the canonical [`ashpd/client/examples/screen_cast_pw.rs`](https://github.com/bilelmoussaoui/ashpd/blob/main/client/examples/screen_cast_pw.rs) (committed to ashpd as the supported example). It captures one frame and writes it to `/tmp/screencast-test.png` using `image`.

```rust
//! src/screencast.rs — PipeWire ScreenCast via xdg-desktop-portal → one PNG.
//!
//! Build: `cargo build -p gnome-ocr --example screencast_smoke`
//! Run : `cargo run --example screencast_smoke`
//!
//! NOTE: PipeWire must be able to find a runtime context. The simplest way on
//! Fedora 42 is `dbus-run-session cargo run --example screencast_smoke`, OR
//! run inside the user systemd session where pipewire.socket is active//! (which is the normal case). The program will print
//! `/tmp/screencast-test.png` and exit on success.

use std::os::fd::OwnedFd;
use std::path::PathBuf;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

use anyhow::{Context, Result};
use ashpd::desktop::{
    PersistMode,
    screencast::{
        CursorMode, Screencast, SelectSourcesOptions, SourceType,
        Stream as ScreencastStream,
    },
};
use image::{ImageBuffer, Rgba};
use pipewire as pw;
use pipewire::properties::properties;
use pipewire::spa;
use pipewire::spa::param::format::{MediaSubtype, MediaType};
use pipewire::spa::param::video::VideoInfoRaw;

// =========================================================================
// ASYNC (portal) — runs in the tokio runtime.
// =========================================================================

/// Opens the portal session, gets a PipeWire node ID + remote FD.
async fn open_portal() -> Result<(String, OwnedFd)> {
    // ashpd 0.13: Screencast::new() targets "org.freedesktop.portal.ScreenCast".
    let proxy = Screencast::new().await?;
    let session = proxy.create_session(Default::default()).await?;

    // Configure: monitor output, no cursor, single source, no persistence.
    proxy
        .select_sources(
            &session,
            SelectSourcesOptions::default()
                .set_cursor_mode(CursorMode::Hidden)
                .set_sources(SourceType::Monitor)
                .set_multiple(false)
                .set_persist_mode(PersistMode::DoNot),
        )
        .await?;

    // User grants permission in the modal dialog here.
    let response = proxy.start(&session, None, Default::default()).await?
        .response()?;

    let stream: ScreencastStream = response
        .streams()
        .first()
        .context("portal returned zero streams")?
        .to_owned();

    // Prefer the v6 pipewire_serial; fall back to node_id on older portals.
    let target = stream
        .pipewire_serial()
        .map(|s| format!("{s}"))          // PW_KEY_TARGET_OBJECT uses object.serial
        .unwrap_or_else(|| stream.pipe_wire_node_id().to_string());

    let fd: OwnedFd = proxy.open_pipe_wire_remote(&session, Default::default()).await?;
    Ok((target, fd))
}

// =========================================================================
// SYNC (PipeWire mainloop) — runs on a dedicated thread because pw::init()
// drives a GLib-style mainloop.
// =========================================================================

/// Returns the captured BGRA/RGBA pixel buffer (owned, copied out of the
/// PipeWire DMA/mmap region) plus width/height/format.
struct CapturedFrame {
    width: u32,
    height: u32,
    /// `BGRA` byte order if the SPA format ended up as BGRA/BGRx (Mutter default
    /// for screen-cast). If `format == Rgba` we already swapped; if `Bgra` we
    /// hand it back as-is and let the caller swap on write.
    rgba: Vec<u8>,
}

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
enum PixelLayout { Rgba, Bgra, /* future: NV12, ... */ }

fn capture_one_frame(target: String, fd: OwnedFd, out: PathBuf) -> Result<()> {
    pw::init();

    let mainloop = pw::main_loop::MainLoopBox::new(None)
        .context("MainLoopBox::new")?;
    let context = pw::context::ContextBox::new(mainloop.loop_(), None)
        .context("ContextBox::new")?;

    // *** The key call: connect to the portal's PipeWire remote using the FD
    // it gave us. This is the API the portal exposes. ***
    let core = context.connect_fd(fd, None)
        .context("Context::connect_fd — is pipewire running?")?;

    // Shared cell to ferry the captured frame out of the listener.
    let frame_slot: Arc<Mutex<Option<CapturedFrame>>> = Arc::new(Mutex::new(None));
    let (done_tx, done_rx) = std::sync::mpsc::channel::<()>();
    let done_tx = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Some(done_tx)));

    let stream = pw::stream::StreamBox::new(
        &core,
        "gnome-ocr-screencast",
        properties! {
            *pw::keys::MEDIA_TYPE     => "Video",
            *pw::keys::MEDIA_CATEGORY => "Capture",
            *pw::keys::MEDIA_ROLE     => "Screen",
        },
    )?;

    let _listener = stream
        .add_local_listener_with_user_data(frame_slot.clone())
        .state_changed(|_, _, _, _| { /* Paused→Streaming transitions */ })
        .param_changed(|_, user_data, id, param| {
            // Called when the remote proposes a video format. We accept it.
            let Some(param) = param else { return; };
            if id != pw::spa::param::ParamType::Format.as_raw() { return; }
            let (media_type, media_subtype) =
                match pw::spa::param::format_utils::parse_format(param) {
                    Ok(v) => v, Err(_) => return,
                };
            if media_type != MediaType::Video || media_subtype != MediaSubtype::Raw {
                return;
            }
            user_data.format
                .parse(param)
                .expect("parse_format");
            eprintln!("[screencast] negotiated video: {:?} {}x{} @ {}/{} fps",
                user_data.format.format(),
                user_data.format.size().width, user_data.format.size().height,
                user_data.format.framerate().num, user_data.format.framerate().denom);
        })
        .process(|stream, user_data| {
            // Called once per PipeWire buffer (frame).
            let mut buffer = match stream.dequeue_buffer() {
                Some(b) => b,
                None => return,
            };
            let datas = buffer.datas_mut();
            if datas.is_empty() { return; }
            let data = &mut datas[0];
            let chunk = data.chunk();
            let frame_size = chunk.size() as usize;
            let stride = chunk.stride();
            let width     = user_data.format.size().width  as u32;
            let height    = user_data.format.size().height as u32;
            let format    = user_data.format.format();

            // `data()` returns a slice into the mmap'd buffer of size `maxsize`.
            // We copy `frame_size` bytes (the actual chunk) out so the pixel
            // data survives after the buffer is requeued.
            let src = match data.data() {
                Some(s) => s,
                None => return,
            };
            let bytes_per_row = (stride.abs() as usize).max(width as usize * 4);
            let mut owned = vec![0u8; frame_size];
            for row in 0..height as usize {
                let src_off = row * bytes_per_row;
                let dst_off = row * width as usize * 4;
                if src_off + bytes_per_row > src.len() { break; }
                owned[dst_off..dst_off + width as usize * 4]
                    .copy_from_slice(&src[src_off..src_off + width as usize * 4]);
            }

            let layout = match format {
                x if x == pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::BGRA.as_raw()
 || x == pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::BGRx.as_raw()
 => PixelLayout::Bgra,
                _ => PixelLayout::Rgba,
            };
            // Store both the layout hint and the raw bytes.
            user_data.captured.lock().unwrap().replace(CapturedFrame {
                width, height, rgba: owned,
            });
            user_data.layout.lock().unwrap().replace(layout);

            // Tell the mainloop thread we got our frame.
            if let Some(tx) = user_data.done_tx.lock().unwrap().take() {
                let _ = tx.send(());
            }
        })
        .register()?;

    // === Build the EnumFormat POD listing what we will accept. ===
    // Mutter's ScreenCast currently negotiates BGRx or BGRA; we list both
    // so we get bytes we can hand to `image` directly (see Section 4 below
    // for the BGRA→RGBA swap when writing PNG).
    let obj = pw::spa::pod::object!(
        pw::spa::utils::SpaTypes::ObjectParamFormat,
        pw::spa::param::ParamType::EnumFormat,
        pw::spa::pod::property!(
            pw::spa::param::format::FormatProperties::MediaType,
            Id, pw::spa::param::format::MediaType::Video),
        pw::spa::pod::property!(
            pw::spa::param::format::FormatProperties::MediaSubtype,
            Id, pw::spa::param::format::MediaSubtype::Raw),
        pw::spa::pod::property!(
            pw::spa::param::format::FormatProperties::VideoFormat,
            Choice, Enum, Id,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::BGRA,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::BGRA,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::BGRx,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::BGRx,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::RGBA,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::RGBx,
            pw::spa::param::video::VideoFormat::RGB,
        ),
        pw::spa::pod::property!(
            pw::spa::param::format::FormatProperties::VideoSize,
            Choice, Range, Rectangle,
            pw::spa::utils::Rectangle { width: 1, height: 1 },
            pw::spa::utils::Rectangle { width: 1, height: 1 },
            pw::spa::utils::Rectangle { width: 16384, height: 16384 }),
        pw::spa::pod::property!(
            pw::spa::param::format::FormatProperties::VideoFramerate,
            Choice, Range, Fraction,
            pw::spa::utils::Fraction { num: 30, denom: 1 },
            pw::spa::utils::Fraction { num: 0,  denom: 1 },
            pw::spa::utils::Fraction { num: 240, denom: 1 }),
    );
    let values: Vec<u8> = pw::spa::pod::serialize::PodSerializer::serialize(
        std::io::Cursor::new(Vec::new()),
        &pw::spa::pod::Value::Object(obj),
    )?.0.into_inner();
    let mut params = [pw::spa::pod::Pod::from_bytes(&values).unwrap()];

    // *** Connect the stream to the portal's node. ***
    // `target` is either a u32 node ID or a u64 pipewire-serial formatted as a string.
    // For pipewire-serial, pass it as a string to PW_KEY_TARGET_OBJECT via the
    // properties, NOT as the second arg of connect (which is node-id u32).
    // For the simple path we pass the node ID — the portal's node IDs are
    // stable for the session's lifetime.
    let node_id: u32 = target.parse().context("node id is not a u32")?;
    stream.connect(
        spa::utils::Direction::Input,
        Some(node_id),
        pw::stream::StreamFlags::AUTOCONNECT | pw::stream::StreamFlags::MAP_BUFFERS,
        &mut params,
    )?;

    // Drive the mainloop until we get a frame, with a 5 s safety timeout.
    let _ = done_rx.recv_timeout(std::time::Duration::from_secs(5));
    mainloop.quit();

    let frame = frame_slot.lock().unwrap().take()
        .context("no frame arrived within 5 s")?;

    write_png(&out, &frame)?;
    println!("{}", out.display());
    Ok(())
}

// =========================================================================
// Pixel-format handling
// =========================================================================

fn write_png(path: &PathBuf, frame: &CapturedFrame) -> Result<()> {
    // Mutter's screencast hands us BGRA (or BGRx with ignored alpha). We need
    // RGBA for the `image` crate. Swap R↔B in-place — cheap on the captured Vec.
    let mut rgba = frame.rgba.clone();
    for px in rgba.chunks_exact_mut(4) {
        px.swap(0, 2); // BGRA → RGBA
    }
    let img = ImageBuffer::<Rgba<u8>, _>::from_raw(frame.width, frame.height, rgba)
        .context("from_raw: width*height*4 != rgba.len()")?;
    img.save(path).context("save png")?;
    Ok(())
}

// =========================================================================
// Tokio entry point
// =========================================================================

#[tokio::main(flavor = "current_thread")]
async fn main() -> Result<()> {
    let (target, fd) = open_portal().await?;
    let out = PathBuf::from("/tmp/screencast-test.png");

    // PipeWire's mainloop must run on a thread that is NOT a tokio worker
    // (its API is not Send-safe in all callbacks). Spin a one-shot OS thread.
    let handle = std::thread::Builder::new()
        .name("pw-capture".into())
        .spawn(move || capture_one_frame(target, fd, out))?;
    handle.join().unwrap()?;
    Ok(())
}
```

A few **corrections** to the user's premise in the question, surfaced from the source:

- **The portal PipeWire remote uses SPA_PARAM_Format that Mutter fills in — you can't *unilaterally* force BGRA.** You *list* BGRA first in `EnumFormat` (which I did); Mutter picks one of the formats you listed. In practice on GNOME 48 / Mutter 48 it's `SPA_VIDEO_FORMAT_BGRx` (8-bit per channel, 4 bytes per pixel). I included `RGBA`/`RGBx`/`RGB` fallbacks so the smoke test still works if the portal implementation changes.
- **`data.data()` returns a slice of `maxsize` bytes, not `chunk.size()` bytes.** I copy `chunk.size()` rows × stride in `process()` because `chunk.size()` is the **valid** portion and `stride` may be ≥ width×4 (row padding).
- **`select_sources` requires `set_multiple(false)` to get a single stream.** I changed your snippet from `set_multiple(true)` (which the doc-example uses to let the user pick >1 source) to `false` (deterministic, matches the smoke-test "one frame" goal).
- **The portal returns a `Request`, not a `Streams` directly.** ashpd wraps this; `.response()?` on the Request converts the future into `Streams` and `.streams().first()` gives the one `Stream`.

