Actually, when an extension registers a D-Bus object on the session bus, the sender of the method call is the unique bus name assigned to the connection. We need to know this name, or we can use a different approach.

The common pattern in GNOME Shell extensions is to use `Gio.bus_get(Gio.BusType.SESSION, ...)` and get the unique name. Then the Rust client calls the method using that unique name as the destination.

But the unique name changes each time the extension is loaded. So we need a way to discover it.

One approach: use `Gio.bus_own_name` to request a specific well-known name like `dev.local.gnome_ocr.Overlay`. Then the Rust client connects to that well-known name.

Let me update the extension to claim the well-known name:

```javascript
this._busNameId = Gio.DBus.session.own_name(DBUS_NAME, Gio.BusNameOwnerFlags.NONE, (conn, name) => {
    log(`[gnome-ocr-overlay] acquired name ${name}`);
    // Register object
    this._registerObject();
}, null);
```

This way, the Rust client always uses `dev.local.gnome_ocr.Overlay` as the destination.

Let me also think about the proxy. In zbus 4:

```rust
#[dbus_proxy(
    interface = "dev.local.gnome_ocr.Overlay",
    default_service = "dev.local.gnome_ocr.Overlay",
    default_path = "/dev/local/gnome_ocr/Overlay"
