parts.text:
The problem: after `.with(file_layer)`, the registry type becomes `Layered<Filtered<...>, Registry>` and then `.with(stderr_layer)` expects `Layer<Registry>` — the second layer needs to implement `Layer<Layered<...>>`, which per-layer filters don't.

The trick: add the second layer BEFORE filtering, i.e. `.with(file_layer).with(stderr_layer)` doesn't work. Instead, put the registry first: stack unfiltered fmt layers then apply filter once. But that was the original failing case (different writer types).

Real canonical solution: use `.with(file_layer).with(stderr_layer)` where both are `fmt::layer()` WITHOUT per-layer filter, and put the EnvFilter on the registry — that's my first attempt, which failed due to differing types (File vs Stderr writer) making the Layer types differ.

The standard fix for that: `.with(file_layer.boxed()).with(stderr_layer.boxed())` — I tried boxing and it complained about `dyn Layer<Layered<Box<_>,_>>`. The problem was I boxed AFTER registry layering created Layered types...

Actually the real issue with boxing: `Box<dyn Layer<S>>` where S is the registry — but after `.with(filter)` the S changed. Let me box and NOT apply registry-level filter:

```rust
let file_layer = tracing_subscriber::fmt::layer().with_ansi(false).with_writer(file).boxed();
let stderr_layer = tracing_subscriber::fmt::layer().with_writer(std::io::stderr).boxed();
tracing_subscriber::registry()
    .with(filter)
    .with(file_layer)
    .with(stderr_layer)
    .try_init();
```

Boxed layers are `Box<dyn Layer<Registry> + Send + Sync>` which is Sized and implements Layer<S> for any S? No — `Box<dyn Layer<Registry>>` implements `Layer<S>` where S=Registry only.

Hmm. This is the classic tracing-subscriber friction.

Simplest reliable solution: avoid two layers. Use a single `fmt` subscriber whose writer fans out to both file and stderr. I can write a tiny fan-out writer:

```rust
struct Tee {
    file: std::sync::Mutex<std::fs::File>,
    stderr: std::io::Stderr,
}

impl std::io::Write for Tee {
    fn write(&mut self, buf: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<usize> {
        self.stderr.write_all(buf)?;
        self.file.lock().expect("log file lock").write_all(buf)?;
        Ok(buf.len())
    }
    fn flush(&mut self) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        self.stderr.flush()?;
        self.file.lock().expect("log file lock").flush()
    }
}
```

Then:
```rust
tracing_subscriber::fmt()
    .with_env_filter(filter)
    .with_ansi(false)
    .with_writer(Tee { ... })
    .try_init();
```
Wait, ansi false for stderr too — minor. Actually with a single writer I can use ansi true and it won't matter much. Let me use ansi false for clean log files; stderr loses color but that's fine.

This is clean, single layer, no trait gymnastics. Let me do that.

Actually simpler still — do I really need stderr AND file? For a GUI app, the file is what matters. But having stderr is useful for running from terminal. The Tee is ~15 lines and gives both. Let me implement Tee.

Let me write it.

Note: tracing_subscriber fmt's MakeWriter for a Tee value — `.with_writer(tee)` where tee: Tee needs to impl `MakeWriter`. `impl MakeWriter for Tee` — there's a blanket impl for `W: Write`? Actually `tracing_subscriber::fmt::writer::MakeWriterExt::with_writer` accepts `MakeWriter`. There's `impl<F> MakeWriter for F where F: Fn() -> W`. For a single value, you can use `MakeWriter::new(writer)` which requires `W: Write`. Let me use `.with_writer(tracing_subscriber::fmt::writer::MakeWriter::new(tee))`.

Let me write it.

Let me rewrite init_logging with the Tee writer.

Also handle the no-file case: writer = stderr only. I can make Tee optional: if file open fails, use a writer that only does stderr. Let me just have two code paths but each with a SINGLE subscriber (no layering):

```rust
match log_file().and_then(open_log_file) {
    Some(file) => {
        let tee = Tee::new(file);
        tracing_subscriber::fmt().with_env_filter(filter).with_ansi(false).with_writer(MakeWriter::new(tee)).try_init();
    }
    None => {
        tracing_subscriber::fmt().with_env_filter(filter).try_init();
    }
}
```

Clean. Let me write it.

Let me also make sure to verify build after.

