EXCELLENT. This is authoritative documentation from the installed headers. Let me read the key part:

> **Buffer negotiation**: "After completing the format negotiation, PipeWire will allocate and notify the stream of the buffers... After the buffers are negotiated, the stream will transition to the PW_STREAM_STATE_PAUSED state."
>
> **Streaming**: "From the PW_STREAM_STATE_PAUSED state, the stream can be set to the PW_STREAM_STATE_STREAMING state **by the PipeWire server when data transport is started**."

So:
- PAUSED = format negotiation done, buffers allocated, ready
- STREAMING = set **by the PipeWire server** when data transport starts

**CRITICAL INSIGHT**: "the stream can be set to the PW_STREAM_STATE_STREAMING state **by the PipeWire server** when data transport is started."

So the client CANNOT force it. The SERVER (the node/session manager) must start data transport. And "data transport is started" means the **producer node starts producing**.

So for a screencast node: the node must start producing frames. That requires the node to be **started** — which happens when the portal session's node is activated.

Now — the key question: what makes the screencast node start?

Let me read more of the header, particularly around the connection section and the "add_buffer" / "param_changed" flow, and importantly what the CLIENT must do.

Let me read the header section before line 98 — the connection section (sec_connect) — and the part about param_changed.

Let me read lines 40-100.

This is the authoritative doc. Let me read it fully.

Also important: my log showed the stream reached PAUSED. Per the docs, PAUSED means "format negotiation completed, buffers allocated"! So **the format WAS negotiated** — the node DID respond! But my `param_changed` callback didn't log "negotiated"...

Hmm! That's a contradiction. Let me re-read.

"After completing the format negotiation, PipeWire will allocate and notify the stream of the buffers... After the buffers are negotiated, the stream will transition to the PW_STREAM_STATE_PAUSED state."

So PAUSED implies format negotiation happened. Which means `param_changed` with SPA_PARAM_Format SHOULD have fired.

But my log had no "negotiated" line!

